course: dictionary
lesson: 4493
title: Hatter: a maker, a saying and a name
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hatter is a person who makes or sells hats. Read the words around it in «I'm a hatter.» and you can guess it is a job, because a job is what someone does. Check by swapping in "a person who makes hats": the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Some words can be names too. A capital letter can show that a word names one special character, such as the Mad Hatter. In «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» by Lewis Carroll, the Hatter hosts a crazy tea party, so here the word is a name, not a job.
explain: Sayings mean something different from their words. «As mad as a hatter» does not mean someone works with hats; the saying is about being very crazy or silly. When you meet a phrase like this, ask what the whole phrase means, not just each word.
fact: A hatter is a person who makes or sells hats.
fact: «As mad as a hatter» means very crazy or silly.
fact: The Mad Hatter is a character in «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» by Lewis Carroll.
fact: A capital letter at the start of a word can show a name, such as the Mad Hatter.
fact: An idiom is a phrase whose meaning is not the same as its separate words, so read the whole phrase to understand it.
exercise: In «I'm a hatter.», what is a hatter? | A very crazy person | A type of clock | A kind of tea party | *A person who makes or sells hats
exercise: «As mad as a hatter» means what? | Very tall and thin | Good at sewing | Tired after a long day | *Very crazy or silly
exercise: In «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland», what is the Mad Hatter? | A kind of tea cup | *A character in the book by Lewis Carroll | The name of Alice's cat | A real hatter who sells hats in the town
test: Alice snaps «Nobody asked your opinion», and the Hatter asks «Who's making personal remarks now?» What does the Hatter mean? | He is asking for a cup of tea | He wants to be her friend | The clock has stopped | *She has done the very thing she scolded him for
test: When Alice asks the answer to the riddle, the Hatter says he has not the slightest idea. What does this mean? | He knows the answer very well | He is going to ask Alice a riddle | *He does not know the answer at all | He has a tiny idea
exam: After everyone moves one place on, where does Alice end up? | In a hat shop | At the Queen's palace | At the Hatter's clean cup | *At the March Hare's messy place, where the milk-jug has been knocked over
